Below is a **ready‑to‑use** helper written in JavaScript (Node v14+).  
It pulls all “game” records for a given calendar year from a RESTful service that exposes a `/games` endpoint – e.g.:

```
GET https://example-sports-api.com/games?year=2019
```

> ⚠️ Replace `https://example-sports-api.com` with the real base URL of the provider you’re working against.  
> Many public sports APIs (e.g. *sportsdata.io*, *balldontlie* for basketball, *football‑data.org* for soccer) expose similar endpoints.  

---

### 📦 Dependencies

```bash
npm install node-fetch@^3   # ES‑module friendly fetch implementation
```

*(If you prefer Axios, just swap out the import.)*

---

### 🧩 Function Code

```js
import fetch from 'node-fetch';

/**
 * Fetches all games played during a particular calendar year.
 *
 * @param {number|string} year - Four‑digit year (e.g., 2019).
 * @param {Object} options Optional configuration:
 *   • apiKey      : String | undefined — API key/token if needed.
 *   • baseUrl     : String          — Base URL of the sports API.
 *   • debug       : Boolean         — Log raw response when true.
 *
 * @returns {Promise<Array<Object>>}
 *   Resolves to an array where each element represents one game record.
 */
export async function getGamesByYear(year, {
  apiKey = null,
  baseUrl = 'https://example-sports-api.com',
  debug = false,
} = {}) {

  const url = new URL(`${baseUrl}/games`);
  url.searchParams.set('year', year);

  const headers = {
    Accept: 'application/json',
    ...(apiKey && { Authorization: `Bearer ${apiKey}` }),
  };

  try {
    const res = await fetch(url.toString(), { headers });

    if (!res.ok) {
      throw new Error(`API error (${res.status}): ${await res.text()}`);
    }

    const json = await res.json();

    /* Some providers wrap the payload inside a top‑level object;
       adjust this mapping according to their schema. */
    const gamesArray =
        Array.isArray(json)
            ? json
            : Array.isArray(json.data)
                ? json.data
                : [];

    if (debug) console.log(JSON.stringify(gamesArray, null, 2));

    return gamesArray;
  } catch (err) {
    console.error(`[getGamesByYear] Failed to fetch games for ${year}:`, err);
    throw err;
  }
}

/*
Example Usage:

(async () => {
  const games2019 = await getGamesByYear(2019, {
    apiKey: process.env.SPORTS_API_KEY,
    baseUrl: 'https://api.soccerstats.com/v1',
    debug: false,
  });
  console.log(`Fetched ${games2019.length} games from 2019.`);
})();
*/
```

#### How it works

| Step | What happens |
|------|--------------|
| 1️⃣ Build URL | Adds the `year` query param (`?year=2019`). |
| 2️⃣ Headers | Sets `Accept: application/json`. Includes bearer auth if supplied. |
| 3️⃣ Request | Uses `fetch()` to perform the GET. |
| 4️⃣ Response check | Throws if status isn’t 200–299. |
| 5️⃣ Parse & map | Converts body into plain JS objects; normalises nested structures. |
| 6️⃣ Return | Gives back an array of game objects ready for consumption. |

---

### 🔑 Things To Keep In Mind

| Topic | Recommendation |
|-------|----------------|
| **Rate limits** | Most APIs throttle per minute/hour. Add retry/backoff logic if you’ll make many calls. |
| **Pagination** | If the provider paginates, loop through pages until `nextPageToken` is exhausted. |
| **Data shape** | Inspect the returned JSON once to confirm field names (`homeTeam`, `awayTeam`, `score`, etc.). Adjust the mapper accordingly. |
| **Error handling** | Wrap callers in `try/catch`; log meaningful messages. |
| **Environment variables** | Store secrets (`SPORTS_API_KEY`) outside source control. Use `.env` + `dotenv` package if desired. |

---

Feel free to drop a comment if you’d like help wiring this up against a concrete API (e.g. *Football‑Data.org*, *NBA Stats*, *ESPN Fantasy Sports*) or adding pagination support!
